{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}The ladder of clients{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>A ladder of clients</h1>
    <p class="lead">The same provider, the same identity, six clients. From one rung to the next, one thing
        changes and one only: how the client authenticates, which way the authorization response comes
        back, what its access token is made of, and what a logout reaches. The last rung asks for
        everything FAPI 2.0 has.</p>

    <ol class="ladder">
        {% for slug, rung in rungs %}
            <li class="card">
                <h2>{{ loop.index }}. {{ rung.name }} <code>{{ rung.client }}</code></h2>
                <p>{{ rung.adds }}</p>
                <table class="kv">
                    {% for what, how in rung.capabilities %}
                        <tr><th>{{ what }}</th><td>{{ how }}</td></tr>
                    {% endfor %}
                </table>
                <p><a class="button" href="{{ path('app_ladder_profile', {rung: slug}) }}">Sign in with this client</a></p>
            </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ol>

    <h2>What has no browser</h2>
    <p>Some flows of the provider are never clicked, because no browser of this application goes into
        them: the client is a program, or it is the provider that calls the client back. The ones below
        are played from the command line in the provider's repository, and each one asserts every step.</p>
    <table class="kv">
        <tr>
            <th><code>castor demo --ciba</code></th>
            <td>A client asks for a user it names and waits (CIBA Core 1.0). That user finds the request
                on <code>/approvals</code> of the provider, reads there the message the client wrote, and
                authorizes it. Two delivery modes, because a client registers only one: the one where the
                client asks again until it has been answered, and the one where the provider calls it back
                on a listener the client opened.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <th><code>castor demo --assertion</code></th>
            <td>An assertion signed by an issuer the tenant trusts, exchanged for an access token for the
                user it names (RFC 7523 §2.1). Nobody signed in, so there is no ID token: the subject of
                the assertion is a user of the tenant through a linked identity and through nothing
                else.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <th><code>castor demo --native</code></th>
            <td>A program installed on the machine of whoever runs it, which listens on the port the
                system gives it and receives the authorization response on the loopback interface
                (RFC 8252 §7.3). The walk plays it twice, on two ports, against a single
                registration.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>What this provider serves</h2>
    <p>The six rungs run against <code>{{ provider_uri }}</code>, with no particular configuration: what
        each client registered is what decides what it receives.</p>
    <p class="note">The last rung is not an <code>oidc_login</code> firewall: the pushed and signed
        request, the signed response, the bound token and the encrypted documents are what Symfony's
        authenticator cannot do yet (<code>RP-04</code>, <code>RP-05</code>, <code>RP-06</code> and
        <code>RP-16</code> of the Symfony status document). It is written here, in this laboratory
        application, and it closes none of those gaps.</p>
{% endblock %}
